$DIR/main.p:23:10: the memory allocated from a is read here while the task started at main.p:18 may write it
    The task started at main.p:18 writes the memory allocated from a at
    pluto/arena/fixed.p:90, in (*Fixed).Reset called at main.p:20, and s waits
    for it at main.p:24. Meanwhile the memory allocated from a is read at
    main.p:23 in main, so the two may run at the same time, one of them writing:
    a data race.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Wait for the tasks before using the memory allocated from a:
        s.Wait()
    Or lock one sync.Mutex around every use of it, in the tasks and here:
        mu.Lock()
        ...
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Scoped tasks" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3402" for a longer explanation)
